I had always had a real interest in film  and I've always been really fascinated  by sets so becoming a set decorator was  kind of a natural progression  I started making super 8 films in high  school and that led to undergraduate  study at NYU and this was in the early  80s and New York was really just  abundant with independent film and  non-union low-budget  pictures so it's kind of like a perfect  time to be educated there and I think it  was by my junior year I was allowed to  access this bulletin board on campus  where all these indie films would put up  their their cards looking for PAS and  positions to fill and the object was  always to be the first one there so you  could see the what shows had gone up and  try to get hired before the next guy so  pretty much my last two years of college  were spent in turning on as many films  as I could be a part of and I kept  finding myself when asked choosing to  work for the art departments because I  was starting to feel like that was the  that was the creative hub behind the  film and it was where everybody that was  involved seemed to be creating the the  world that I wanted to be a part of it  seemed like the the place to have to  have fun and to be involved and fulfill  this desire to work on film that led to  me getting my first decorating gig in  the mid-80s I think simply because I was  willing to do it for two hundred and  fifty dollars a week and basically I did  I was the decorator and the construction  coordinator and I painted and dressed  and it led to many more jobs that were  the same way I I think I worked every  position in the art department before  kind of honing in on the decorator as  the position that I admired and more  to pursue I think a lot of people feel  that a set decorator is akin to an  interior decorator and that we are  mostly responsible for having taste and  and doing the shopping and well that  certainly is a part of it it's really  only a small part of what the decorator  does I don't think people are fully  aware of how faceted our position really  is we we really are historians and  anthropologists sociologists I've heard  my my peers refer to this position in in  many different ways but we kind of take  the script and we mine it for details  and we look for ways to to enhance the  environments that of that are part of  our story  in ways that perhaps haven't been  thought of or discovered yet so it  involves a lot of a lot of research a  lot of reading between the lines and  investigating what's not necessarily on  the page to to fully develop a world so  it goes beyond supplying furniture or  objects for a room it and it it really  goes a lot deeper into understanding  every environment in the film and what  those environments need to relay in  order for them to to facilitate the  vision of the film I think the set  decorator is affected by the new  technology in a very subtle way I think  that over the years I see that the tasks  that I am called upon to facilitate on a  show evolving and changing with the  technology but the core the core tasks  that I'm expected to fulfill seem to  remain constant  you could be working on a show that is  3d or involves a being shot on high-def  video or a show that's largely CGI and  all these elements we have to become  proficient in in this language and in  that particular way of seeing and of  making a film but at the same time we  still have a script to break down we  still have to either fabricate or find  the objects with which to address the  sets and and facilitate the the vision  of the film so really every movie is a  crash course in whatever that subject is  that that you have to become adept at  the language of in order to make the  movie so I've learned a great deal over  in the past decade alone of regarding  all the new technologies but I think it  really hasn't changed the way that I do  my job  you
 

